I now have sufficient evidence to issue my comprehensive review. Here is my verdict:

```json
{
  "verdict": "revise",
  "scores": {
    "novelty": {
      "score": 4,
      "rationale": "Independently verified via 'Scripts Through Time' survey (arXiv:2604.18722), 'When Less Language is More' (arXiv:2505.15257), and OpenReview searches: no existing work combines transliteration-based causal experiments with formal Bayesian cross-classified mediation analysis and sensitivity batteries to decompose multilingual reasoning gaps. The closest competitors ('When Less Language is More' uses representation ablation, not input-level transliteration; 'What Makes Good Multilingual Reasoning' uses logistic regression, not formal causal decomposition) are clearly differentiated."
    },
    "feasibility": {
      "score": 3,
      "rationale": "CRITICAL: RomanLens (arXiv:2502.07424) itself explicitly reports that Chinese does NOT exhibit latent romanization ('we do not observe Latent Romanization in Chinese, likely due to its logographic script'), yet the proposal cites RomanLens's KL<0.01 finding without noting this Chinese exclusion while including Chinese among target languages. The 'Scripts Through Time' survey (arXiv:2604.18722) confirms 'transliterating into a phonetic script like Latin removes crucial semantic and contextual nuances' for logographic languages. Arabic/Hebrew vowelization via uroman is also lossy. These violate the core 'content-preserving transliteration' assumption for 3-5 of the 15-20 target languages. Cost estimates are roughly plausible (H100 spot at $1.65-2.39/GPU-hr on Vast.ai verified), though the stated $4-5/hr underestimates a 4×H100 cluster ($6.60-9.56/hr). Tools (uroman, IndicXlit, brms) are all verified as available."
    },
    "clarity": {
      "score": 4,
      "rationale": "Exceptionally well-structured proposal with clear two-strategy design, comprehensive landscape review, and detailed experimental specification. The triangulation logic (experimental + observational + supplementary RDD) is lucidly explained. Minor clarity gaps: benchmark-language overlap not explicitly tabulated (which of the 15-20 target languages appear in which benchmarks), and the 1,800+ observation count assumes complete language×model×benchmark coverage which is unrealistic."
    },
    "impact": {
      "score": 4,
      "rationale": "Answering 'how much of the multilingual reasoning gap is causally due to tokenization' has direct implications for whether tokenizer redesign alone can close gaps, informing both research priorities and industry resource allocation. The causal framing elevates this beyond the many correlational studies. If tokenization accounts for only 20-30% (vs. data scarcity), this redirects effort toward data-centric approaches; if 50%+, it justifies massive tokenizer R&D investment."
    }
  },
  "issues": [
    "CRITICAL: Romanization of logographic scripts (Chinese, Japanese kanji) is semantically lossy—Chinese pinyin creates massive homophone ambiguity (e.g., 'shi' maps to dozens of characters), and the 'Scripts Through Time' survey (arXiv:2604.18722) explicitly notes that 'transliterating [Chinese] into a phonetic script removes crucial semantic and contextual nuances.' RomanLens (arXiv:2502.07424) itself found Chinese does NOT exhibit latent romanization, yet the proposal cites RomanLens's KL<0.01 without disclosing this Chinese exclusion. This violates the core assumption that transliteration 'preserves language, grammar, semantics, and cultural content' for Chinese/Japanese, making the (a)→(b) contrast capture both tokenization AND comprehension effects for these languages.",
    "MODERATE: Arabic and Hebrew romanization via uroman is acknowledged by uroman's own documentation as producing unvowelized output for scripts lacking diacritics, creating genuine comprehension ambiguity beyond tokenization change. This partially confounds the causal instrument for these 2+ languages.",
    "MINOR: Miles et al. cited as 'Journal of Causal Inference 2020' but actually published in 2017 (J. Causal Inference, Vol. 5, Issue 2, Feb 28, 2017; PMC confirms this). Li & Beretvas cited as 2013 for cross-classified multilevel mediation but the relevant paper ('Testing mediation effects in cross-classified multilevel data') appears to be from 2016-2017 in Behavior Research Methods—the 2013 paper addresses sample size limits for upper-level mediation in SEM.",
    "MODERATE: AfriMMLU covers 16 African languages (mostly Latin-script: Yoruba, Hausa, Swahili, etc.) with limited non-Latin overlap (mainly Amharic in Ge'ez). MGSM covers 10 languages (Chinese, Japanese, Thai, Bengali, Telugu overlap with target). PolyMath covers 18 languages (Arabic, Bengali, Chinese, Japanese, Korean, Thai, Telugu overlap). The proposal should explicitly tabulate which benchmarks cover which target languages—current framing implies broad coverage but several target languages (Georgian, Armenian, Malayalam, Gujarati, Hebrew) may have NO benchmark coverage.",
    "MINOR: The 1,800+ observation count for Strategy 2 assumes complete language×model×benchmark coverage (50+ languages × 12 models × 3 benchmarks), but in reality many cells will be missing (e.g., Georgian has no MGSM/PolyMath data; BLOOM 7B likely underperforms too severely on reasoning tasks). A realistic estimate accounting for missing data would likely be 800-1,200 observations."
  ],
  "feedback": "The core research question and two-strategy design are strong—keep them. The key fix needed is handling logographic and consonantal scripts properly: (1) Partition target languages into 'clean instrument' (alphabetic/abugida: Hindi, Bengali, Tamil, Telugu, Malayalam, Gujarati, Thai, Korean, Georgian, Armenian—where romanization is near-lossless) and 'confounded instrument' (logographic: Chinese, Japanese; consonantal: Arabic, Hebrew, Urdu—where romanization loses semantic information). Run the primary causal analysis only on the clean-instrument languages (~10-12 languages), and treat confounded-instrument languages as a separate analysis group where the transliteration effect bounds the COMBINED tokenization+information-loss channel. (2) Stop citing RomanLens KL<0.01 as universal—explicitly note it excludes Chinese and was tested on only 7 languages. (3) Add a benchmark-language coverage table showing exactly which target languages appear in which benchmarks, and revise the observation count for Strategy 2 accordingly. (4) Fix citation dates: Miles et al. 2017, verify Li & Beretvas year. (5) For the mediation analysis, discuss how the crossed random-effects structure handles the many missing cells in the language×model×benchmark design matrix. These revisions are feasible within the existing timeline and would substantially strengthen causal identification claims.",
  "reason": "Novel and well-designed causal decomposition study, but the core 'content-preserving transliteration' assumption is violated for logographic/consonantal scripts (Chinese, Japanese, Arabic), requiring language partitioning and selective citation correction before the causal identification strategy is internally valid."
}
